Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; cold northern light; Buck a massive mixed St. Bernard–shepherd, 140 lbs, rich brown coat with white chest splash and brown muzzle markings — identity locked for all ages of the story

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE - SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Sun-kissed lawns, poplars, a wide veranda. BUCK — broad-breasted, long-furred, white splash down his chest — races across the grass with laughing children, plunges into a cement swimming tank, shakes water in a glittering spray.

(SOUND: children laughing, water splashing, distant dogs barking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing. Men had found a yellow metal in the Northland. These men wanted dogs.

EXT. ORCHARD PATH - NIGHT

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, leads Buck on what seems a stroll. Money chinks between Manuel and a rough STRANGER at a little flag station.

(SOUND: coins chinking, night insects, distant train whistle)

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles stout rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls. The rope tightens. He springs; the stranger throws him, chokes him senseless.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR / SAN FRANCISCO SHED - NIGHT

Jolting darkness. Buck's jaws close on the kidnapper's hand. Later, in a shed behind a saloon, men file off his brass collar and fling him into a cagelike crate.

(SOUND: locomotive shriek, crate wood scraping, men cursing)

KIDNAPPER
All I get is fifty for it. An' I wouldn't do it over for a thousand, cold cash.

EXT. HIGH-WALLED BACK YARD - SEATTLE - DAY

Four men set the crate down. THE MAN IN THE RED SWEATER — stout, sweater sagging at the neck — signs a book, takes up a hatchet and a club.

(SOUND: hatchet splitting wood, men scrambling onto the wall)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck launches one hundred and forty pounds of fury. The club checks him mid-air. Again and again he charges; again and again the club smashes him down. Blood from nose and mouth. A final shrewd blow. He crumples, senseless.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you.

He brings water and raw meat. Buck drinks and eats from the hand that beat him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver — a master to be obeyed. That club was his introduction to the reign of primitive law.

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

Cold air. Buck and good-natured CURLY, a Newfoundland, stand with PERRAULT — little, weazened, French-Canadian — and FRANCOIS — black-faced half-breed giant. White mush falls from the sky. Buck sniffs, licks — it bites like fire and is gone.

(SOUND: ship's propeller throb fading, wind, men laughing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

Chaos of dogs and men. Curly makes friendly advances to a husky. A flash of teeth. Her face rips open. Thirty huskies close in a silent circle. She goes down screaming under the mass.

(SOUND: snarls, yelps, Curly's agony, axe and clubs scattering dogs)

Francois swings an axe; men with clubs drive them off. Curly lies limp in bloody snow. SPITZ — big, snow-white — runs out his scarlet tongue, laughing. Buck's eyes fix on him with deathless hatred.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you.

EXT. TRAIL AND CAMP - NORTHLAND - DAY/NIGHT

Harness straps buckle on Buck. He hauls firewood, learns "ho" and "mush" under Dave the wheeler's nips and Spitz's jerks. Night: driven from the tent, he discovers Billee curled under the snow, digs his own hole, sleeps.

(SOUND: whip crack, sled runners, wind over snow, Buck digging)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He swiftly lost the fastidiousness of his old life. Under the law of club and fang, he stole when hunger compelled him. The domesticated generations fell from him. On still cold nights he pointed his nose at a star and howled long and wolflike.

EXT. SHORE OF LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Bleak camp. Spitz steals Buck's warm nest. Buck springs. They circle, snarling — then starving camp huskies pour in, a skeleton horde. Pandemonium. Buck tastes warm blood. Spitz attacks him treacherously from the side. The team flees onto the ice.

(SOUND: clubs on ribs, yelping horde, grub-box capsizing, wind knife-sharp)

FRANCOIS
Ah, my frien's — mebbe it mek you mad dog, dose many bites!

EXT. FROZEN TRAIL - DAY

Days of toil. Buck's soft feet bleed; Francois rubs them, cuts moccasins from his own tops. Dolly goes mad; Buck races for his life until Francois's axe ends her. Spitz tears Buck's flesh in the aftermath — and takes Francois's worst whipping.

(SOUND: ice cracking under runners, axe blow, whip lash)

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. Some dam fine day heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow. Sure. I know.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - MOONLIGHT - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit. The whole team and fifty police dogs in full cry. Buck leads, sounding the old wolf-cry. Spitz cuts across, breaks the rabbit's back. Buck drives into him shoulder to shoulder.

(SOUND: pack in cry, rabbit shriek, then sudden ghostly quiet — only panting breaths)

They circle in powdery snow. Silent wolfish ring waits. Fang clashes fang. Buck rushes low, crunches Spitz's left foreleg — then the right. Spitz staggers on three legs. The circle tightens.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck was inexorable. Mercy was a thing reserved for gentler climes.

Buck springs. Shoulder meets shoulder. The dark circle becomes a dot on the moon-flooded snow as Spitz disappears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on — the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. CAMP AT DAWN - DAY

Spitz is gone. Buck, covered in wounds, trotts to the lead position. Francois tries Sol-leks there. Buck drives Sol-leks back. Club raised — Buck remembers the red sweater, circles just out of range, refusing any place but the front.

(SOUND: traces jingling, Francois's angry shouts, club thudding on snow when thrown)

FRANCOIS
Eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois obeys. Buck swings into the lead. The sled breaks out. They dash onto the river trail.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar!

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - MAIL RUN - DAY

Heavy loads, endless miles. DAVE, the wheel dog, grows morose, cries out in the traces. Taken out, he bites through Sol-leks's traces to reclaim his place. He pulls till he can no longer rise. A revolver shot rings behind a belt of timber.

(SOUND: sled bells, Dave's broken howl, single gunshot, then whips and merry bells again)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck knew, and every dog knew, what had taken place behind the belt of river trees.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET / TRAIL SOUTH - DAY

Worn to skeletons, the team is sold to HAL — callow, belted with cartridges — CHARLES — watery-eyed, fierce mustache — and MERCEDES — soft, chattering. Overloaded sled. Whips. Outside dogs die. Billee is axed in the traces. Spring murmurs while they stagger like wayfarers to death.

(SOUND: whip whistling, Mercedes weeping, ice groaning, club on bone)

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - MOUTH OF WHITE RIVER - DAY

Dogs drop as if struck dead. JOHN THORNTON — practical, kind-eyed, limping slightly — whittles an axe-handle. Hal clubs Buck, who will not rise.

(SOUND: club impacts dull and distant, Mercedes's hysterical scream, ice shifting far out)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton knocks the knife away with the axe-handle, cuts Buck's traces. Hal's sled crawls onto the ice — then the back end drops. Mercedes screams. A yawning hole. Gone.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. RIVER BANK CAMP - SPRING - DAY

Buck heals. SKEET the setter washes his wounds. NIG the big black dog romps. Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his hands, shakes him, murmurs loving oaths. Buck's teeth close carefully on Thornton's hand — a caress.

(SOUND: river running, birdsong, Thornton's low laugh)

JOHN THORNTON
God! you can all but speak!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. Thornton alone held him. Yet deep in the forest a call was sounding.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON STREET - DAWSON - DAY

Sixty below. A sled loaded with a thousand pounds of flour, runners frozen fast. Crowd banks around. MATTHEWSON's gold sack hits the bar. Thornton kneels, cheek to Buck's cheek.

(SOUND: crowd murmur dying to silence, cold air cracking, breath misting)

JOHN THORNTON
(whispering)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, Buck. Gee!

Buck swings right — crackling under the runners.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Left — the sled breaks out.

JOHN THORNTON
Mush!

Buck's body knots. Claws scar the snow. Inch by inch the sled moves — then steadily. A hundred yards. The crowd roars. Thornton falls to his knees, shaking Buck, cursing him softly and lovingly.

(SOUND: runners grating free, then a rising cheer exploding into a roar)

EXT. WILDERNESS TRAIL AND SPRUCE CAMP - MONTHS - DAY/NIGHT

East after a lost mine. Gold heaped in moose-hide bags. Buck muses by the fire — visions of a short-legged hairy man. The call grows. He runs with a lean timber wolf, sniffs noses, then returns to Thornton in a frenzy of affection.

(SOUND: pan washing gravel, fire crackle, distant wolf howl, Buck's answering whine)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Each day mankind slipped farther from him. But as often as he gained the green shade, the love for John Thornton drew him back.

EXT. FOREST AND CAMP CLEARING - DAY

Buck pulls down a great bull moose after four days. Racing home, he finds Nig dead with an arrow, Hans feathered like a porcupine, Pete killed in his blankets. YEEHAT hunters dance about the wrecked lodge.

(SOUND: triumphant Yeehat chant, then Buck's roar — neither bark nor yelp)

Buck becomes a live hurricane. Throat after throat. Panic. He drives them screaming into the woods. At the pool's edge he scents Thornton — no trail leads away.

(SOUND: arrows hissing, men screaming, then awful silence but for wind and water)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

EXT. MOONLIT CLEARING - NIGHT

A wolf pack pours in like a silvery flood. Buck stands motionless. The boldest leaps — neck broken. Others slash and fall back. At last a long lean gray wolf advances; they touch noses — the wild brother. An old battle-scarred wolf howls. Buck sits and howls. Then he runs with them, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he runs.

(SOUND: pack yelps rising to the long wolf howl — Buck's voice joins, deeper, fuller)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The years were not many when the Yeehats noted a change in the breed of timber wolves — splashes of brown on head and muzzle, a rift of white down the chest. They tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack.

EXT. OPEN SPACE AMONG TREES - SUMMER DAY / WINTER NIGHT

A great, gloriously coated wolf stands where a yellow stream once sank from rotted moose-hide sacks. He howls once, long and mournfully. Then — winter — he runs at the head of the pack through pale moonlight, leaping gigantic above his fellows, great throat a-bellow.

(MUSIC: lone mournful howl blooming into the full song of the pack)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
